#{[17592186527132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527132 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/4030ed55caa6900e6927021a1b4819d4fd2157b803fc23ccc05165ba01bd3fce.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "128afa31-6089-55bb-b4c5-d34347e7a11c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527132 #edb/ref 1074 "This is the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ that's been translated $3$ units *left*.\n\n![](images/d9/d933bb153126e39091c73df703facc27e149fb793021eb4b38a5456b76d42bb1.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-(x\\,+\\,3)^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527141 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/46d382b4d31818e63f917b8df074a5710e187362fc1aaa125a28245a371564cd.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26a8d25b-e6dd-5460-9414-80558f8bec9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527141 #edb/ref 1074 "This is the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ that's been translated \n\n- $2$ units to the *right*, and\n\n- $1$ unit *up*.\n\n![](images/4d/4d0840ff427022b8bf23ebd3d36d58bf0e5bbe75d339f139ed86930d53eec09d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-(x\\,-\\,2)^{3}\\,+\\,1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527145 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527145 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/74718361ade9a3a1a5123cbebe26baf2b49a6ae4739b544fe5b41578bd71d038.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b8be0e4-6ba2-573c-9c5f-c0fd730c101c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527145 #edb/ref 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated \n\n- $2$ units to the *right*, and\n\n- $2$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/c6/c652e1639e68ecc5f09fccce36ed236e7bad3f99d704e95c40286afa5db66963.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=(x\\,-\\,2)^{3}\\,-\\,2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527155 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527155 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7d/7d341093cf35425a75174a6871d452c38daa0d281cdc2a2b7aaceeca390dee5b.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is the graph of $y={x}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f99401c-4f2a-57bb-bccb-f67190e5a3b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527155 #edb/ref 1074 "First, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y={x}^{3}$ increases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this increasing property are II and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs II and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y={x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=-1.$ Substituting $x=-1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=(-1)^{3}=-1,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(-1,-1).$ Only graph IV satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph IV corresponds to the graph of $y={x}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527162 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527162 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527162 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527162 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/05/05173f7d9b4cdf89d4167f71533da6b95942bf6355bc6245fc95fbd7c434440e.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above show the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527162 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4aa71abb-1f45-5c68-95b9-e97d2bf13015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527162 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that graph III resembles a straight line. So, we disregard this option.\n\nNow, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are I and II.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs I and II. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-{x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(0,0).$ Only graph II satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph II corresponds to the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527164 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f17474f6837ba685a64fa7681df485c2495f38ed789355361b4117b814b9dbc.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51849fa8-ba8b-5524-9925-28a75279e347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527164 #edb/ref 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated $2$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/60/60daebed5585a53e1e3154ba568bd4baa4b223cb6cb7fe95ebffda291e23e9a6.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y={x}^{3}\\,-\\,2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527174 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-(x-3)^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "818b8208-c98b-5ec3-a330-6bbba9ff0a1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527174 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have $-3$ inside the brackets, we translate the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}$ by $3$ units to the *right*. \n\nSo, the graph of $y=-(x-3)^{3}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e4/e4afdd5a6c2b466b9447f7c9c0d0e4c885f17aa219e557bf9589bff88aeea0ae.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527211 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527211 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527211 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/04/04cb5fcb2ea4013f64d76439a882730455f77908c9e885f52f262efce7bccb58.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527211 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3b4690a-a388-56c8-b51f-66b065fce082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527211 #edb/ref 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated $1$ unit *down*.\n\n![](images/18/1827cffb455aeea03512c208f1cd2914ce669abc62a27dd56cb52c29bf8094c4.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y={x}^{3}\\,-\\,1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527216 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d20da3acdbbf1f08a5c0713ce2fe0313a345319accfa1b18efbc87f46705faa8.png)\n\nThe graph above is a translation of $y={x}^{3}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d93e70df-0d5a-518e-ab33-73b00d22f653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527216 #edb/ref 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated $6$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/07/078aa5c391829a1544d0f83c51c32e0653312f2b06158200ffc82dd8cd784330.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y={x}^{3}+6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
